  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bullpen let the game get away in one inning, and the bats couldn't recover from it.",
    "what_this_means": "Dodgers hit V\u00e1squez for five runs in the sixth and never looked back. Hart didn't last beyond the second, leaving the staff scrambling before the game was even close. Sheets went 3-for-4 with a home run and drove in two, and Jackson Merrill added two hits, but nine hits across the lineup scored only three times in RISP situations. The math didn't work. You don't come back from a twelve-run hole when you're leaving runners on base in nearly every inning. This team got to two on with two outs in the eighth and came away empty. The kind of at-bat that should have stayed with them. It didn't matter anymore. The game was decided the moment Marinaccio and Dur\u00e1n started leaking runs in the fifth and sixth, and nothing after that could touch it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Inconsistency hit hard. The pitching setup looked fine for four innings, then collapsed into the kind of inning that reverses an entire night. Nine runs allowed before a single out was recorded in the sixth. The offense showed some life with Sheets and Merrill connecting, but it was scattered and too late. You need both sides to work together; you need the order to matter. Here, the pitching broke first, the offense came alive second, and the gap between those moments was too wide to close. One clean inning from the bullpen changes everything. They didn't get it. They've now fallen back to nine games behind in the division, and the trend is pointing down hard. This is what happens when the pen doesn't hold and the bats are too slow to answer.",
